<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/foundational/forms-of-government">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every government answers two questions: <strong>who decides?</strong> and <strong>who
    benefits?</strong> Aristotle spotted the 2×2 twenty-three centuries ago — rule by one, few
    or many, each in a common-good form and a self-serving form. Political science still uses
    his grid as the entry point, updated with a modern axis: the <strong>democracy–autocracy
    spectrum</strong>, scored by whether leaders are chosen in competitive elections and held to
    account.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from the Athenian assembly to a 968-million-voter election — milestones of rule by the people" widget="timeline" config={govTimeline} />

  <h2>The modern families</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Liberal democracy</strong>: competitive elections plus courts, press and rights that bind the winners too. The "liberal" half is what distinguishes it from mere majorities.</li>
    <li><strong>Parliamentary vs presidential</strong>: two ways to split the top job — fuse it with the legislature (UK, India) or elect it separately (US, Brazil). Both are democracies; they fail differently.</li>
    <li><strong>Autocracy</strong>: one person or party decides without competitive accountability. Modern versions keep elections as theatre — the competition is what they remove.</li>
    <li><strong>Monarchy and theocracy</strong>: legitimacy from bloodline or divine authority; most surviving monarchies are constitutional figureheads, with real power in elected hands.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the measures actually count</h2>
  <p>
    Indices like V-Dem and Freedom House score observable things: were elections free? can
    opposition campaign? do courts overrule executives? is the press harassment-free? The
    takeaway for a student: <em>"democracy" is not a yes/no flag but a bundle of checkable
    practices</em> — which is why it can erode gradually, election by election, without a single
    dramatic coup.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Aristotle, Politics; V-Dem Institute annual reports; Freedom House; Election Commission of India (2024).</p>
</TopicPage>
